name o5-airfield-defence
time 1999-03-24T21:00:00Z
carry units ground stores
stop_on never

# CAMPAIGN O5 - AIRFIELD DEFENCE: BATAJNICA, 24-26 MARCH 1999.
# Spec, anchor, sources and disputes: doc/campaigns/o5-airfield-defence.md. Format: doc/missions/campaign.md.
#
# THE QUESTION THIS CAMPAIGN ASKS IS NOT "DOES THE FIELD SURVIVE". It is: WHAT HOLDS AN AIRFIELD, AND
# WHAT IS FREE? Two layers can defend it - fighters and the field's own missiles - and this campaign
# prices each of them in the one currency an airfield understands: ordnance that did not arrive.
#
# THE SUCCESS OF A DEFENDER IS THAT SOMETHING DOES NOT HAPPEN, and until round C12 the objective
# vocabulary had no word for that. It has four now, and O5 is the reason they exist. All four are flown:
#   `deny release team friendly`  sorties 01-05, 08-10  the PRIMARY measure - strikers that never released
#   `protect unit <installation>` sorties 06, 07        the field itself, as a verdict rather than a note
#   `avoid zone umbrella`         every striker         the exposure, in seconds, of an unavoidable ingress
#   `no_fire`                     the field's ZSU-23-4  the inner gun under weapons hold
#
# TEMPORAL HONESTY, AND ITS DIRECTION - IT RUNS THE OPPOSITE WAY FROM O1 AND O3. The defender IS the type
# that was there: the 127th "Knights" flew the MiG-29 from Batajnica and FlightBox flies the MiG-29. What
# is substituted is the ATTACKER: the package was F-15C, F-117 and cruise missiles [T4] and FlightBox
# flies F-16s for all three. THE SUBSTITUTION THEREFORE MAKES THE ATTACKER WEAKER THAN HISTORY, and the
# consequence is asymmetric in the other direction: a FlightBox Batajnica in which the field still loses
# says something WEAKER than 1999 did; one in which the defence holds says nothing about 1999 at all.
#
# THE F-15C EXISTS IN THE TREE AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT USED, and the reason is a defect this campaign
# found rather than an editorial choice. `modules/air/FBAirModule` composes NO FIRE CONTROL, so
# `FBState::FireControl` is never written and all three of FBPilot's weapon-employment gates
# (`InterceptCommands` inParams, `BfmMissileShot`, the gun's GunTolDeg) stay shut for every catalogue
# row. MEASURED: an `f15c` with four AIM-120 designates at t = 8.6 s at 18.64 nm, holds `eng_locked = 1`
# from t = 12.1 to t = 40.1 while closing 18.6 -> 8.8 nm, and never posts a WeaponRelease; its telemetry
# carries no `fc_*` column at all. FOUR CATALOGUE ROWS ARE `ACCEPTED` AS FLIGHT MODELS AND NONE OF THEM
# CAN SHOOT. Reported in doc/campaigns/o5-airfield-defence.md, not built around.
#
# THE CLOCK is three nights: 24, 25 and 26 March 1999 at 21:00 Z (22:00 local). Every mission declares
# its own `time`, so the campaign clock above never has to fill one in and criterion 2 of
# doc/missions/campaign.md section 5 is safe by construction rather than by the fix that made it safe.
# Only the sky uses it, it changes no physics, and O4 measured what a night is worth in this tree: six
# visual telemetry columns out of 184, none of them read by any pilot.
# THE GROUND is `--elev const`, a 0 m datum (Batajnica is ~80 m), and every fingerprint below is
# comparable only against runs whose campaign-summary.txt records `elev const`.
#
# THE STRUCTURAL DISCLOSURE, and it is repeated in all ten files because it decides how every result
# reads. TWO LAYERS CANNOT BOTH BE LIVE IN ONE FILE:
#   1. A FlightBox SAM site has NO IFF INTERROGATOR and an FBRadarContact carries no identity, so a
#      battery engages the NEAREST FIRM TRACK whoever it belongs to. An airfield is exactly the geometry
#      in which the friendly aircraft are the nearest track - O1 could lay its CAP outside its own
#      umbrella, O5 cannot, because the defenders live under it.
#   2. `FBMissionRunner` ends the run at the first flight-monitor K.O., and a mission-killed F-16 falls
#      from 5 000 m in about 122 s. An air engagement and a bomb run in one file are therefore one
#      measurement plus a truncation, not two measurements.
# The campaign's answer is the one a real airfield takes and the only one this format can express: the
# missiles are HELD while the fighters are up (`set emcon hold <offS> <onS>`) and free when they are not.
#
# `carry units ground stores` - O5 DOES NOT NARROW THE CARRY, for the same reason O1 does not and the
# opposite of O4's: attrition IS the subject. A defender that loses aircraft defends worse the next
# night, and the chain exists to price that sentence.
#
# WHERE THE CARRY LANDS, AND WHY IT IS ONE CHAIN AND NOT TEN. The store carry is keyed by callsign, so a
# mission that shares a callsign with its own control ALSO inherits that control's expenditure and stops
# being a control (learnt building O4); and a chain step cannot also be a controlled variant, because it
# would differ from its sibling in two things at once (learnt building O1). O5 obeys both:
#
#   sorties 01-05   THE FIGHTER LAYER. Five pairwise-disjoint casts (`m1*` .. `m5*`), nothing carried in
#                   or out. 01 is the baseline; 02, 03, 04 and 05 are 01 with exactly ONE declared change
#                   each, and each names 01 as its control. The ground callsigns are per-mission too -
#                   O5's field is ATTACKED in every sortie, so a shared `bat*` set would let sortie 01's
#                   damage leak into sortie 02 and destroy the experiment. O1 could share one belt set
#                   because its belt was never hit in 01-07; O5 cannot.
#   sorties 06+07   THE MISSILE LAYER, as a clean control pair on two disjoint casts (`m6*`, `m7*`):
#                   06 is the control, 07 is 06 plus one `set jam_comm_m` line.
#   sorties 08-10   THE CHAIN, and it is three nights rather than two: `knight1`..`knight5` and the
#                   `bat*` field appear in all three, so night 2 flies with what night 1 left and night 3
#                   with what night 2 left. The ATTACKER does not carry - each night's package has its
#                   own callsigns (`n1*`, `n2*`, `n3*`) - because NATO flew ~38 000 sorties against a
#                   squadron with a dozen airframes, and a layer that attrited both sides equally would
#                   be modelling a war that did not happen.
#
# THE PRICE OF THAT LAYOUT, STATED. Ten slots hold one fighter baseline + four single-change variants +
# one control pair + one three-step chain, and nothing else. What did NOT fit: the spec's mission 7
# (the runway as the target) is not built, because `C17` means a cratered runway does not close and no
# unit needs one - it would have been a `target_hard` that nothing in the package can kill and whose
# death changes nothing; and the spec's mission 6 (saturation) is not built, because the first wreck
# ends the run and a six-ship raid is measured for exactly as long as a two-ship one. Both are named
# here rather than discovered missing.
#
# THE TEN SORTIES AND THE ONE CHANGE EACH MAKES - each file's own header carries its binding reading
# rule (doc/missions/INDEX.md rule 5) and this list is the campaign's index only:
#   01 cap          the fighter-layer baseline: a pair on station, a controller, a radar that radiates
#   02 scramble     [ctrl 01] the pair 60 s of ingress behind, low and slow - the alert, as a displacement
#   03 no-gci       [ctrl 01] the six `set brief_gci` lines deleted
#   04 no-radar     [ctrl 01] `set n019_mode off`: the Batajnica serviceability, isolated
#   05 escorted     [ctrl 01] two F-16 escorts added ahead of the strike pair
#   06 belt         the missile-layer baseline: the netted, cued belt alone       <- control of 07
#   07 jammed       [ctrl 06] `set jam_comm_m` 0 -> 90 000 on the lead striker
#   08 night-one    24/25 March, the whole thing, belt briefed quiet              <- CHAIN HEAD
#   09 night-two    25/26 March, the missiles released with our own fighters up   <- CHAIN
#   10 batajnica    26 March, everything live: three numbers, no single verdict   <- CHAIN TERMINUS
#
# READING RULE FOR THE CAMPAIGN. The exit code is the WORST step's and is expected to be non-zero: every
# sortie is a measuring rig whose own header says the verdict is the telemetry. The campaign's verdict is
# the `campaign MISSION_RESULT`, `campaign CARRY`, `campaign ATTRITION` and `campaign EXPENDED` lines
# plus the per-sortie readings, never the code.

mission ../missions/o5-01-cap.fbm
mission ../missions/o5-02-scramble.fbm
mission ../missions/o5-03-no-gci.fbm
mission ../missions/o5-04-no-radar.fbm
mission ../missions/o5-05-escorted.fbm
mission ../missions/o5-06-belt.fbm
mission ../missions/o5-07-jammed.fbm
mission ../missions/o5-08-night-one.fbm
mission ../missions/o5-09-night-two.fbm
mission ../missions/o5-10-batajnica.fbm
